  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 253 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: More shower activity is expected today as a \nsurface trough and upper level trough both cross our area. Not \nexpecting any thunderstorms or severe weather. Coverage and \nintensity should be less than yesterday. Winds will become more \nnorthwesterly as the day goes on as trough axis pushes east of \nour forecast area. Drier air will then work into the region, and\nremain through Wednesday night with surface and upper level \nridges building over the area. More shower activity is expected \non Thursday into Thursday night, and lingering into Friday as \nwell. A surface cold front along with some upper level shortwave\nenergy will cross the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Following a frontal passage and parent low \ndeparture Thursday and Friday, the weekend looks to trend drier \nas our flow pattern becomes more zonal. There still looks to be \nsome signs of subtle cyclonic flow as the upper low lifts north \nand east, which may spur some isolated shower activity in \neastern Ontario. Some of which could bring some increase cloud \ncover and an isolated shower to the St. Lawrence Valley (though \nPoPs are still below 20%). Temperatures will generally be \nseasonable in the 70s, though a few spot low 80s will be \npossible in the southern, wider valleys by Sunday. Humidity will\nalso gradually increase through the weekend from the upper 40s \non Saturday to the upper 50s by Monday. The increase in humidity\nwill be a result of a trough swinging through the area to start\nnext week. Models diverge on the strength of this trough with \nthe GFS ensembles denoting a continue more zonal pattern, \nwhereas the Euro ensembles depict a slightly more amplified \ntrough, which is in line with the CMC as well. The forecast will\nlikely change in the coming days as models resolve this feature\nbetter in the days to come. As of right now, most of the area \nhas a 30-40% chance for showers Monday, with temperatures in the\nupper 70s which does seem to be on the warmer side of most \nguidance. There is a good chance for conditions to be quite a \nbit cooler early next week should the pattern become more \namplified with additional cloud cover and daytime precipitation.",
